Revision history for EEL

{{$NEXT}}
    - Support separate Actions objects, make semantic values and .made/.ast
      projections of published Supply results.
    - Evaluate dynamic ** { ... } repetition bounds once per entry, preserving
      them across forks and validating integer counts and inclusive ranges.

0.2  2026-11-05T23:17:03Z
    - Include exact numeric intervals in adaptive cross-field intersection,
      caching distinct bounds and preserving coercive numeric fallback.
    - Index literal ends-with predicates with reverse Unicode tries, sharing
      prefix-trie cleanup and conservative fallback semantics.
    - Index literal starts-with string predicates with per-field Unicode prefix
      tries, pruning unused nodes and preserving custom-method fallback.
    - Intersect anchor hits with cached cross-field restrictions; adaptively
      skip low-yield secondary checks and expose pruning counters.
    - Index field presence and canonical defined predicates, with conservative
      handling of custom objects and shared dotted-field resolution.
    - Preserve event-id across successive emissions of the same recognition
      candidate; distinct starts retain distinct identities.
    - Select largest, smallest (?) or all (!) complete repetition matches per
      input while preserving overlapping continuations; support ** suffixes.
    - Allow nullable entry patterns with consuming paths and use exclusive
      event-time deadlines. Do not flush losing repetition matches at EOF.

0.1  2026-11-05T03:50:38Z
    - Initial release of EEL, an event pattern language for Raku Supplies.
    - Add event and pattern declarations with captures, recursion, proto/multi
      dispatch, alternatives, repetition and interleaving.
    - Support partitioning, event-time windows, lookahead and absence detection.
    - Provide Supply.match with object matching and optional event reemission.
    - Add an indexed in-memory runner for active patterns and temporal queries.
    - Provide the eel CLI for JSONL logs, transformations, inline event definitions
      and timestamp-ordered merging of files and live streams.
